```

Implement flat chunking with `chunks_exact_mut` and safe array conversion/`as_chunks_mut`, zipped in a fixed named order; check remainders before use. No unsafe shape casts, pointer reconstitution or truncating `zip`. `ObsBuffersMut` is the literal flat counterpart of the row struct, including length-one scalar slices; spell all fields out in code, not a dynamic string map. `ObsStaging` has the same named fields as owned Vecs and an immutable n_envs. `publish` checks source/destination n_envs equality in **release before any copy**, returning a Shape error with both sizes on mismatch. After that check, admitted field lengths match by construction, all copies are infallible and it returns Ok. A debug assertion alone is insufficient because two independently valid buffer sets can have different E. Task 1.4 also admits this equality before candidate work so it cannot newly fail during final publication.

Validate config and staging capacities once at construction. Validate incoming NumPy **dtype, full shape, C-contiguity, writable borrow, disjoint byte ranges and checked length products on every binding call**: Python may substitute arrays, even when most calls reuse the same ones. Do not cache raw addresses across calls. Rust scalar types guarantee dtype after borrowing; flat lengths are checked once per batch call, not inside every seat loop. Each per-step `prepare()` checks state ranges, applicable sentinels and finite derived floats in **release**, not only `debug_assert!`. `check_row` scans ranges, sentinel applicability, zero padding, ranks, contiguous masks, count/context/mask agreement and finite floats; use it in tests/debug and optional diagnostic builds. Release validity follows preflight + typed writing; do not scan all outputs twice in the release hot path. Python `check_contract` remains admission/test-only, and all successful test batches must pass it.

